# Analysis and Dating of the Passage

Let me work through the clues systematically:

## Key Evidence

**1. Linebender projects** — Linebender (Vello, Xilem, etc.) was founded around 2022-2023, so this must be after that.

**2. C++26 features mentioned** — "stackful coroutines," "embed binary file contents to initialize arrays" (std::embed), "safe range-based for loop." These were being discussed for C++26 in the 2023-2024 timeframe. The passage says C++26 "will likely have" these, suggesting the standard wasn't finalized (C++26 is expected ~2026).

**3. "C++20 brings us modules... looking forward to it actually being implemented fairly soon"** — This is a joke about modules' poor compiler support, which was a running theme in 2023-2024.

**4. Rust features mentioned** — "generic associated types" (stabilized in Rust 1.65, **October 2022**) are described as "recently landed." "Type alias impl trait" is described as "forthcoming" (it stabilized in Rust 1.86, **April 2025**). This places the writing between late 2022 and early 2025.

**5. Herb Sutter's cppfront** — Announced in **September 2022**; Carbon was announced in **July 2022**. Both are referenced as recent developments.

**6. "Stroustrup's paper on safety"** — This refers to Stroustrup's "Profiles" safety paper, which was published in **September 2023** (P2739), in response to the White House ONCD report pressure and NSA guidance on memory-safe languages.

**7. The author** — The details (GUI infrastructure in Rust, font parsing CVEs in Android, Linebender) strongly suggest **Raph Levien**, who announced exactly this kind of transition. He published a blog post about moving Linebender toward C++ in **early April 2025**.

## The Twist

However, I should note the passage has hallmarks of being **satire or an April Fools' joke**: the absurd arguments (build systems including "DEC OSF/1 AXP," dismissing memory safety because "customers will understand it's not our fault," praising the C++ subreddit's "sense of humor," the "politics-free" language section) read like a parody of Rust-to-C++ migration announcements.

## Best Guess

**April 1, 2025** — This matches Raph Levien's actual April Fools' Day 2025 blog post announcing (jokingly) that Linebender was moving to C++. All the technical references (GATs landed, TAIT forthcoming, Stroustrup's 2023 safety paper, C++26 proposals) are consistent with that date. [end of text]


